# 7. Latent Factor

Introduce

[
F_i\sim N(0,1).
]

Conditional on

[
(F_i,X),
]

assume all forms are independent.

This does **not** imply unconditional independence.

The latent factor induces dependence across forms after integration.

Normalize

[
F_i
\sim
N(0,1).
]

Fix one loading to be positive to resolve sign indeterminacy.

---

# 8. Binary Form Model

For binary form

[
j
]

assume

[
B_{ij}
\mid
F_i=f
\sim
Bernoulli(\pi_{ij}(f)).
]

where

[
\pi_{ij}(f)
===========

\Phi
(a_j
+
X_i'\kappa_j
+
\lambda_jf).
]

Estimate

* intercept,
* covariate coefficients,
* factor loading,

for every binary form.

---

# 9. Count Form Model

Initial implementation

Poisson.

Assume

[
C_{ir}
\mid
F_i=f
\sim
Poisson(\nu_{ir}(f))
]

where

[
\nu_{ir}(f)
===========

\exp
(d_r
+
X_i'\xi_r
+
\rho_rf).
]

Estimate

* intercept,
* covariate coefficients,
* factor loading,

for every count form.

The shared latent factor already creates marginal overdispersion.

Therefore implement and validate this model before introducing negative binomial.

The count-family implementation should nevertheless be modular.

Later versions should permit

```text
PoissonCountFamily

NegativeBinomialCountFamily
```

through a common interface.

