# 11. Completer Likelihood

Completers reveal the pricing forms.

Therefore price is observed.

The contribution is

[
L_{i1}
======

s_i(p_i)
\int
\phi(f)
g_i(B_i,C_i|f)
df.
]

The demand probability separates from the factor integral because

once the price is known,

the latent factor has no remaining direct effect on demand.

This follows from the exclusion restriction

forms affect completion only through price.

---

# 12. Non-completer Likelihood

For non-completers

the forms are missing.

The exact likelihood is

[
\int
\phi(f)
\sum_b
\sum_c
g_i(b,c|f)
(1-s_i(p(b,c)))
df.
]

Direct evaluation is impossible because

binary forms require

[
2^{J_B}
]

configurations

and count forms have infinite support.

The goal is therefore to compute exactly the same probability without enumerating every form vector.

---

# 13. Why Price Collapse is Valid

Suppose demand depends on the forms only through total price.

Define

[
S
=

\sum_jt_jB_j
+
\sum_rw_rC_r.
]

Define

[
h(s|f)
======

P(S=s|f).
]

Partition the full form space by total price.

Then

[
\sum_b
\sum_c
g(b,c|f)
q(p(b,c))
=========

\sum_s
h(s|f)
q(p(s)).
]

Nothing is approximated.

This follows directly from

* the law of total probability,
* the law of iterated expectations.

Therefore the problem becomes construction of

[
h(s|f).
]

---

