# 15. Why Convolution Works

Conditional on

[
(F_i,X_i),
]

each form contributes an independent random price increment.

For independent discrete variables

[
U,V,
]

[
P(U+V=s)
========

\sum_u
P(U=u)
P(V=s-u).
]

This identity is convolution.

Since total price is the sum of independent contributions,

its probability distribution is obtained by repeated convolution.

Convolution is therefore not an approximation.

It is an exact probability identity.

---

# 16. Count Price Distribution

Construct the price contribution generated by count forms first.

Let

[
S_C
===

\sum_rw_rC_r.
]

Conditional on

[
F=f,
]

each count is Poisson.

Use the probability generating function

[
E[z^C]
======

\exp(\nu(z-1)).
]

The generating function of the weighted sum equals the product of the individual generating functions because the counts are conditionally independent.

Group count forms sharing identical price increments.

Use the compound-Poisson recursion to recover

[
P(S_C=s).
]

The recursion is obtained by differentiating the generating function and matching coefficients.

Implement through

[
s=0,\ldots,M.
]

This is exact up to the configured truncation point.

---

# 17. Binary Price Convolution

Initialize

the price distribution with the count-generated distribution.

Process one binary form at a time.

Each binary form contributes

either

zero

or

its configured price increment.

Update the current price distribution using the convolution identity.

This sequential dynamic program computes the exact conditional price distribution without enumerating every binary configuration.

The implementation should use two working arrays and alternate between them.

Memory should scale with

[
O(M).
]

---

# 18. Gaussian Quadrature

The remaining integral is over one latent factor.

Approximate

[
\int
\phi(f)
r(f)
df
]

using Gauss-Hermite quadrature.

Transform nodes and weights to the standard normal.

Allow the number of nodes to be configurable.

Reuse node-level probabilities wherever possible.

---

# 19. Tail Truncation

Poisson counts imply infinite support.

Retain

[
0,\ldots,M.
]

Compute

retained probability,

tail probability,

and an upper bound on the approximation error.

Hold

[
M
]

fixed during optimization.

Report tail mass in diagnostics.

---

# 20. Final Observation-Level Likelihood

For completers

evaluate

the observed demand probability

times

the quadrature approximation to the observed form probability.

For non-completers

evaluate

the quadrature approximation to the integrated non-completion probability over the conditional price distribution.

The estimator should maximize the observation-level log likelihood.

Do not replace the latent price distribution with its conditional mean.

In general

[
E[\Lambda(V-\beta P)]
\neq
\Lambda(V-\beta E[P]).
]

The entire conditional price distribution must therefore be retained throughout estimation.

